     "body": "The decades-old study changed how scientists and parents view shy kids. Facebook Share on LinkedIn (Opens in new window) LinkedIn Share on X (Opens in new window) X Flipboard Share on Mail (Opens in new window) Mail Harvard psychologist Jerome Kagan had a rigid view on nature vs nurture early in his career: “I entered psychology because I wanted to discover how powerful the environment was and I was very reluctant to acknowledge the role of any biological differences between children,” he says in an interview with The Brainwaves Video Anthology YouTube channel. But the more he studied upbringing, family life, and other environmental factors, the more he felt he was missing a piece of the bigger picture of what shapes a child’s temperament, whether they are shy or outgoing. Harvard psychologists followed infants and toddlers for years to uncover the roots of shyness Kagan says he could see differences between infants of different nationalities at just 3 months old, which led him to realize that our natural temperament must have a biological underpinning. So in the late 1970s, he launched a series of studies following what he called both “high reactive” and “low reactive” children. Researchers screened infants as young as four months for how much they reacted to unfamiliar stimuli. This could be as simple as introducing a new toy or mobile, or taking them to an unfamiliar place. “High reactive” kids would cry and flail, while “low reactive” kids would be mostly unbothered. The most extreme cases on both ends of the spectrum were selected for long-term follow-ups over the course of several years, with many children being studied well into their adolescence, and some into adulthood. All-in-all, Kagan studied temperament for decades, and much of what he discovered helped write the so-called book on shy kids. Here are a few of the most interesting bits of his research: Watch on YouTube Being “shy” is mostly physiological Kagan found that infants only three or four months old could be easily sorted into the high or low reactive bucket. His research eventually revealed that shyness, or high reactivity, stems from an overactive or hypersensitive nervous system. Physical differences in brain makeup and neurochemistry make some people more attuned to stimulation. Kagan could quite literally see the difference through activity in the amygdala, the part of the brain responsible for our survival instincts and fight-or-flight response. Previous research had pegged shyness as a fixed personality trait, but Kagan’s long-term studies revealed that it is often biologically based and inherited, which is a total game-changer. In some cases, he found that people could “develop” shyness over time due to trauma at home or other environmental factors. But shyness, or being “inhibited,” is not locked in place One of the most fascinating parts of Kagan’s long-term research was how it clearly showed that shyness can change over time. Even though its basis lies in our nervous system and brain chemistry, the children he studied sometimes became less shy and reactive over time—and sometimes more. “I would say, if you start with 10 very shy children at age 1, you’ll end up with 3 who are very shy as adolescents,” he said in 1991, per The Harvard Crimson . The most extreme cases were the least likely to change. Shy kids can learn to be bold adults, but the brain markers remain One major follow-up in Kagan’s work was doing brain scans, some 20 years later, on some of th",
